tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:9:5: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `Aᵀ @ B` is rewritten to `matmul[ta](A, B)`
  |
9 | x = Aᵀ @ B
  |     ^^^^^^
  = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
  = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[ta]` reads it transposed, so the 40·30 element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
  = note: because exact: `matmul[ta]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
  = note: because the flops are unchanged: 48,000

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:10:8: note: [GVN-001] `Aᵀ` at line 10 reuses the value computed at line 9
   |
10 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
   |        ^^
 9 | x = Aᵀ @ B
   |     -- computed here first
   = note: applied by the `gvn` pass
   = note: because the earlier `transpose` has the same operands and runs first on every path to this one, so both compute the same value

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:10:8: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta](A, A)`
   |
10 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
   |        ^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
   = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[ta]` reads it transposed, so the 40·30 element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
   = note: because exact: `matmul[ta]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
   = note: because the flops are unchanged: 72,000

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:10:8: note: [LA-GRAM-001] `Aᵀ @ A` is rewritten to `matmul[ta,alg=syrk](A, A)`
   |
10 | gram = Aᵀ @ A
   |        ^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `structure` pass
   = note: because A is multiplied by its own transpose: entries (i, j) and (j, i) sum the same products (`aₚᵢ·aₚⱼ` and `aₚⱼ·aₚᵢ`, and multiplication commutes) in the same order
   = note: because one triangle is computed and mirrored: 37,200 flops instead of 72,000
   = note: because exact: the result is bit for bit the general product's, and bitwise symmetric (its type gains the Symmetric tag)

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:11:9: note: [LA-TRANSPOSE-001] `A @ Aᵀ` is rewritten to `matmul[tb](A, A)`
   |
11 | outer = A @ Aᵀ
   |         ^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `transposes` pass
   = note: because the transposed operand is a matrix: `matmul[tb]` reads it transposed, so the 40·30 element copy that `transpose` makes is never made
   = note: because exact: `matmul[tb]` is defined as the transpose followed by the product, so every bit and every error is the same; a kernel may read the operand with swapped strides
   = note: because the flops are unchanged: 96,000

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:11:9: note: [LA-GRAM-001] `A @ Aᵀ` is rewritten to `matmul[tb,alg=syrk](A, A)`
   |
11 | outer = A @ Aᵀ
   |         ^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `structure` pass
   = note: because A is multiplied by its own transpose: entries (i, j) and (j, i) sum the same products (`aₚᵢ·aₚⱼ` and `aₚⱼ·aₚᵢ`, and multiplication commutes) in the same order
   = note: because one triangle is computed and mirrored: 49,200 flops instead of 96,000
   = note: because exact: the result is bit for bit the general product's, and bitwise symmetric (its type gains the Symmetric tag)

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:11:13: note: [GVN-001] `Aᵀ` at line 11 reuses the value computed at line 9
   |
11 | outer = A @ Aᵀ
   |             ^^
 9 | x = Aᵀ @ B
   |     -- computed here first
   = note: applied by the `gvn` pass
   = note: because the earlier `transpose` has the same operands and runs first on every path to this one, so both compute the same value

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:12:8: note: [LA-SYMMETRIC-001] `gramᵀ` is rewritten to `gram`
   |
12 | same = transpose(gram)
   |        ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `structure` pass
   = note: because gram is Matrix[30,30] and Symmetric: aᵢⱼ == aⱼᵢ exactly, so its transpose has the same bits
   = note: because exact: no 30·30 element copy is made

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:13:8: note: [LA-DIAGONAL-001] `D @ B` is rewritten to `diag.mul[left](D, B)`
   |
13 | rows = D @ B
   |        ^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `structure` pass
   = note: because D is Matrix[40,40] and Diagonal: every off-diagonal entry is exactly zero, so the product scales the rows of B by its diagonal
   = note: because exact: entry (i, j) is `0.0 + d·x` in either form (the other terms add ±0 to a sum that starts at +0.0), so every bit is the same, including the sign of a zero
   = note: because 800 multiplications instead of 64,000 flops

tests/ir/exact_linalg.sky:14:8: note: [LA-DIAGONAL-001] `B @ E` is rewritten to `diag.mul[right](B, E)`
   |
14 | cols = B @ E
   |        ^^^^^
   = note: applied by the `structure` pass
   = note: because E is Matrix[20,20] and Diagonal: every off-diagonal entry is exactly zero, so the product scales the columns of B by its diagonal
   = note: because exact: entry (i, j) is `0.0 + d·x` in either form (the other terms add ±0 to a sum that starts at +0.0), so every bit is the same, including the sign of a zero
   = note: because 800 multiplications instead of 32,000 flops
